<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seychelles</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Seychelles is an archipelago in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, which was a British colony from the early nineteenth century. The British used it as a place of exile for deposed rulers. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a> note that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asantehene">Asantehene</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prempeh-i">Prempeh I</a> was exiled to the Seychelles after the British occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seychelles.jpg" alt="Seychelles" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vxb83 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Seychelles.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Seychelles is an archipelago of 115 islands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, with its capital and largest city, Victoria, about 1,500 kilometres east of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. The islands lie northeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>. The inner islands, 42 granitic and 2 coralline, occupy the shallow Seychelles Bank and hold 54% of the land area. The granitic islands rest on a submerged granite block, the Seychelles microcontinent, which lay between Madagascar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> before the breakup of Gondwanaland. Alfred Wegener cited this granite as evidence of continental drift.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The islands were uninhabited before sustained outside contact. Arab sailors knew of them through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean-trade">Indian Ocean trade</a> routes, and scholars identify them with the Zarin islands described by al-Mahri. Europeans learnt of them in the early 16th century. The first sighting was made on 15 March 1503 by Thomé Lopes, during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a>'s fourth Portuguese India Armada. The earliest recorded landing was in January 1609, by the crew of the Ascension under Captain Alexander Sharpeigh, on a voyage of the British East India Company.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> began to take control in 1756, when Captain Corneille Nicholas Morphey laid a Stone of Possession on Mahé. The islands were named after the French politician Jean Moreau de Séchelles and formed part of the colony of Isle de France. In August 1770 the ship Thélémaque landed 15 white settlers and 13 African and Indian slaves on Ste. Anne Island. Enslaved Africans were then brought to work the plantations.</p><p>In 1794 the senior administrator, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queau-de-quinssy">Jean-Baptiste Quéau de Quinssy</a>, negotiated with the British frigate HMS Orpheus and kept the islands under French control as neutral territory. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> took control after invading Isle de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in December 1810, formalised by the Treaty of Paris of 1814. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> was prohibited in 1835, and indentured labourers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> were later employed. Seychelles became a separate crown colony from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> in 1903. It gained independence as a republic in 1976, and a coup in 1977 ousted its first president, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-mancham">James Mancham</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Seychelles is a republic whose president is both head of state and head of government. French, English and Seychellois Creole are official languages, and the Creole, a French-based language, is the most widely spoken. The population was estimated at 100,600 in 2022, making it the smallest African country by area and the least populated sovereign African state. Most people descend from African, French, Indian and Chinese immigrants. It belongs to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-union">African Union</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-nations">Commonwealth of Nations</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Seychelles” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1896, in the Fourth Anglo-Ashanti War, a British expedition occupied Kumasi without a fight and deposed Asantehene Prempeh I, who was exiled first to Sierra Leone and from 1900 to the <b>Seychelles</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Kabalega was captured in 1899 together with Mwanga, the deposed king of Buganda, a long-time rival who had become his ally; the British exiled both men to the <b>Seychelles</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bunyoro">Kingdom of Bunyoro · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The French-settled <b>Seychelles</b> surrendered to British warships several times during the Napoleonic Wars, while the French commandant Quéau de Quinssy stayed on to run the islands.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-seychelles">British Seychelles</a></li></ul></section>
